  </p>

  <h2>Viewport &amp; typography cues hint who they optimise for</h2>
  <p>
    The viewport meta tag permits <code>user-scalable=yes</code> up to <code>maximum-scale=3</code>, which is friendlier to low-vision adjustments than locked-zoom malls that accidentally violate WCAG-ish norms.
    Skeleton CSS pairs Latin stacks with <strong>PingFang SC</strong>, <strong>Hiragino Sans GB</strong>, and <strong>Microsoft YaHei</strong>—sensible bilingual ergonomics even while <code>lang=&quot;en-US&quot;</code> anchors marketing HTML.
    Root stylesheet uses fluid <code>vw</code>-scaled font sizing before richer breakpoints attach inside downstream bundles.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>UX takeaway:</strong> English-first markup plus Chinese-capable font stacks mirror how cross-border shoppers oscillate languages mid-session; readers juggling Polish or Iberian Discord transcripts still benefit from pinch-zoom allowances when translating screenshots on phones.
  </p>

